-- subject: Migration 265 (drop the `detect_intersections` RPC — flywheel U3 supersession, 2026-08-17). Migration 023 created `detect_intersections(min_strength int, max_results int)`: a SQL function that RE-SCORED item pairs at read time from `operational_scenario_tags` / `compliance_object_tags` overlap plus `related_items` linkage (3 pts/scenario, 2 pts/compliance-object, 5 pts explicit-link, 2 pts both-high-priority). It predates the connection-discovery engine. Since migration 252 and the flywheel wave (U0-U4), `item_cross_references` carries the ONE scoring home's persisted output — `discover.mjs` scores with grounded basis, `write-edges.mjs` / `mint-item.ts` persist it — and the flywheel build plan (`docs/plans/flywheel-build-plan-2026-08-10.md`, U3) names this exact retirement. EVIDENCE: the function's only caller, `/api/admin/intersections`, is re-pointed in the same PR to read `item_cross_references` and assemble pairs via `src/lib/connections/pair-view.mjs`; grep over `src/` and `scripts/` finds no other `.rpc("detect_intersections")` call. ORDERING (two-track policy, CLAUDE.md standing rule 3): this is a DROP whose safety depends on the CONSUMER change, not schema the code depends on — the reverse of the DDL-first case — so it applies AFTER the re-pointed route merges and no deploy window ever runs the old route against a missing function. Migration 023's historical file and migration 160's `search_path` pin are untouched history (the ALTER dies with the function); `intelligence_items.intersection_summary` is NOT part of the RPC's scoring and stays. **APPLIED 2026-08-18** — ordering honoured: PR #467 merged (`93611d4c`) and BOTH production Vercel deploys of that commit reported success BEFORE the DROP ran, so no window existed in which the deployed old route could meet a missing function (the migration header says "after the re-pointed route merges"; merge alone is not sufficient — the deploy is what removes the caller from production, and that distinction was checked, not assumed). Verified BY EXECUTION, not presence, on both sides: (1) pre-drop baseline `SELECT p.proname, pg_get_function_identity_arguments(p.oid) FROM pg_proc p JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid=p.pronamespace WHERE n.nspname='public' AND p.proname='detect_intersections'` returned exactly ONE row with identity args `min_strength integer, max_results integer` — matching the DROP's signature, which is what makes the post-check non-vacuous; (2) the same query post-drop returns ZERO rows. `to_regclass` is deliberately NOT used — it resolves relations only and returns NULL for a function that still exists, so it cannot distinguish "dropped" from "never visible to it" and would pass vacuously here. (3) SURFACE PROOF (the real test that the reader no longer depends on the function): /admin -> Sources -> Intersections loaded against production after the DROP and rendered `Total pairs 200 · Strong (>=0.9) 200 · Medium 0 · Weak 0 · Explicitly linked 0`, with per-pair scores (`1.00`) and grounded basis chips (`Basis (4)` / `Basis (6)`: "grounded in the same source", "both touch ocean-bunkering", "both touch ocean-fuel-blend-mandate", …) plus the engine-weights line; no error state, and a console read filtered for `error|failed|500|404|PGRST|42883` over a full post-drop page load returned nothing. RESIDUAL, recorded not silently dropped: `fsi-app/supabase/seed/verify-intersections.mjs` still calls `.rpc("detect_intersections")` and is now dead — the migration's own evidence line scoped its grep to `src/` and `scripts/`, and this file is under `supabase/seed/`, outside that scope. It is NOT execution-wired (no lane, no npm script, no CI reference — only its own usage comments and a historical audit register mention it), so the DROP turns no lane red; it is retired in the same PR as this status update rather than left as a script that fails on first use. SECOND RESIDUAL, found while checking the first and materially worse: `pair-view.test.mjs` — shipped by #467 as the ONLY proof of the replacement reader — was git-tracked and executed by NOTHING. The npm-dep lane in `.github/workflows/discipline.yml` globs `fsi-app/src/**/*.npmtest.mjs` and names a fixed list; a `.test.mjs` under `src/` matches neither, and the file is referenced nowhere in `.github/` or `.discipline/`. #467's CI was green because the test never ran, not because it passed — the exact "execution over existence" class of standing rule 15, arriving on the same PR that removed the old scoring home. Fixed by renaming to `pair-view.npmtest.mjs`, which is the house wiring mechanism (the workflow's own comment says naming is what wires a proof, and 19 sibling proofs already use that suffix) — no new machinery, no identity change. Now confirmed matched by `git ls-files 'fsi-app/src/**/*.npmtest.mjs'` and passing 8/8 under the new name. [glyph:verbatim]
-- 265 — Drop the detect_intersections RPC: the flywheel U3 supersession retires the second scoring home.
--
-- WHAT GOES, AND WHY.
--
-- Migration 023 created detect_intersections(min_strength int, max_results int): a SQL function that
-- RE-SCORED item pairs at read time from operational_scenario_tags / compliance_object_tags overlap
-- plus related_items linkage (3 pts/scenario, 2 pts/compliance-object, 5 pts explicit-link,
-- 2 pts both-high-priority). It predates the connection-discovery engine. Since migration 252 and the
-- flywheel wave (U0-U4), item_cross_references carries the ONE scoring home's persisted output —
-- discover.mjs scores with grounded basis, write-edges.mjs / mint-item.ts persist it — and the
-- flywheel build plan (docs/plans/flywheel-build-plan-2026-08-10.md, U3) names this exact
-- retirement: "detect_intersections supersession lands here ... retire the RPC's scoring."
--
-- THE EVIDENCE: as of this migration the function's ONLY caller, /api/admin/intersections
-- (route.ts:46), has been re-pointed to read item_cross_references and assemble pairs via
-- src/lib/connections/pair-view.mjs (this PR). grep over src/ and scripts/ finds no other
-- .rpc("detect_intersections") call. The read path this function served is fully replaced; two
-- parallel scoring homes for the same question is exactly the divergence class the single-scoring-home
-- rule exists to prevent.
--
-- ORDERING (two-track policy, CLAUDE.md standing rule 3): this is a DROP whose safety depends on the
-- CONSUMER change, not schema the code depends on — the reverse of the DDL-first case. It applies
-- AFTER the re-pointed route merges, so no deploy window ever runs the old route against a missing
-- function.
--
-- WHAT DELIBERATELY STAYS: migration 023's historical file (history, not live schema);
-- migration 160's search_path pin on this function (160 is history — the ALTER it ran dies with the
-- function); intersection_summary on intelligence_items (item-level prose written at generation,
-- read by the intersections view's cards — not part of the RPC's scoring).

DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS public.detect_intersections(integer, integer);
